<p>{% person "hugh-altnow" %}Dr. Hugh Altnow{% endperson %} built this house in 1912 and lived there until 1924. The mail-order house arrived precut and was shipped through the Bingenheimer Mercantile Company.</p>

<p>The one-and-a-half-story dwelling is a classic Arts and Crafts bungalow. It retains built-in china cabinets and bookcases, box-beam ceilings, original light fixtures, and matching hardware from drawer pulls to doorknobs. Its distinctive foundation is made of prairie stone, while the wide eaves are typical of bungalow design. Hitching posts from the original wooden St. Joseph Church are displayed in the front yard.</p>

<p>Altnow eventually specialized in children's diseases and moved to the Twin Cities, where he helped establish the Hennepin County Clinic in Minneapolis. In 1924, he sold the house to {% person "cecil-smith" %}Dr. Cecil Smith{% endperson %}. Smith served for 25 years as the Northern Pacific Railroad's local physician and as physician for the State Industrial School, now the Youth Correctional Center. He also founded the Custer Health District.</p>

<p>The Smith family occupied the house from 1924 until the death of Smith's wife, {% person "winifred-smith" %}Winifred{% endperson %}, in 1976. {% person "bob-feickert" %}Bob{% endperson %} and {% person "gloria-feickert" %}Gloria Feickert{% endperson %} later purchased it and lived there until 1988. Both worked for the <em>Bismarck Tribune</em>, and Gloria had worked for Dr. Smith while attending high school.</p>
